---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { MotionLab, MotionIntents } from "../../components/MotionLab";
---
<Base title="Motion" description="How Diorama UI moves, the three motion levels, and how to offer people a way to reduce motion or turn it off."><article class="prose-page motion-page">
<h1>Motion that moves<br />only as much as you want.</h1>
<p>Motion is part of how the system feels. It also has to respect the people using it. Every component reads the same motion tokens, so one setting changes how the whole interface moves.</p>

<h2 id="levels">Three levels</h2>
<p>Choose a level below. It applies to this site, and the specimens show what changes.</p>
<MotionLab client:visible />
<div class="motion-table" role="region" aria-label="What each level does" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Full</th><th scope="col">Reduced</th><th scope="col">Off</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Surfaces that slide or grow in</th><td>Move</td><td>Fade in place</td><td>Instant</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Change in place</th><td>Eases</td><td>Eases, briefly</td><td>Instant</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Loops, smooth scrolling, autoplay</th><td>Run</td><td>Stopped</td><td>Stopped</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Dragging something</th><td colspan="3">Always follows your finger</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>With no setting, the system follows the operating system. Someone who asks their device for less motion gets Reduced. Nothing ever depends on motion alone: every animated change also changes a label, a colour or an icon.</p>

<h2 id="in-your-app">Offer the setting in your app</h2>
<p>A level is one attribute. Put it on the <code>html</code> element for the whole app, or on any element for one region, such as a dashboard of live tiles that should stay calm.</p>
<pre class="code-frame">{`<html data-ui-motion="reduced">`}</pre>
<p>Reduced and Off always win, however the attributes nest. Full overrides the operating system, so set it only when a person chose it in your settings, never as a default. Store the choice and apply it before the first paint, so the page never animates once at the wrong level.</p>

<h2 id="javascript">Motion you write in JavaScript</h2>
<p>CSS follows the level on its own. A script asks for the level of the element it is about to move, and listens for changes if it runs for a long time.</p>
<pre class="code-frame">{`import { motionLevel, subscribeMotionLevel } from "@/lib/motion";

if (motionLevel(element) === "full") {
  element.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth" });
}`}</pre>
<p>Never ask the operating system’s setting directly. That answer ignores the setting in your app.</p>

<h2 id="timing">Timing</h2>
<p>Durations and curves are tokens. An intent token pairs a duration with an easing, so a component names the kind of movement instead of picking numbers. Play them side by side; at Reduced and Off the dots stay still, because travel is what those levels remove.</p>
<MotionIntents client:idle />
<p>Something that enters or leaves moves by the travel tokens, never a fixed amount: <code>scale-(--ui-enter-scale)</code> for a popup, and a slide written against <code>--ui-motion-travel</code>. That is what lets Reduced turn a slide into a fade.</p>
<pre class="code-frame">{`data-[starting-style]:translate-y-[calc(100%*var(--ui-motion-travel))]`}</pre>

<p><a href="/components/">Browse components</a> · <a href="/docs/">Get started</a></p>
</article></Base>
